<h2>A case that changed shape</h2>
<p>The FIR began as a missing-person report: the petitioner himself reported his minor daughter missing in May 2023, and the case was first registered under Section 363 IPC. After she returned, her statements and a medical examination led to the addition of Section 376 IPC and Section 6 POCSO against the father. He was arrested on 16 May 2023, chargesheeted on 13 July 2023, and remained in custody through three unsuccessful bail attempts while the trial inched forward before the POCSO court at Patiala House.</p>

<h2>The doctrine: change of circumstances</h2>
<p>Successive bail applications occupy a disciplined middle ground. A fresh plea cannot re-argue what was already rejected; it must point to something new. The court quoted the settled formulation from <em>Kalyan Chandra Sarkar</em> &mdash; dismissal of an earlier application does not preclude a subsequent one where there is a material change &mdash; and found the test amply met:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>PW-1 resiled.</strong> The victim, the prosecution&rsquo;s principal witness, was declared hostile across two depositions and denied the allegations.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>PW-2 followed.</strong> The mother did not support the prosecution and disputed the school-recorded birth year &mdash; placing even the foundational age question in doubt.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The court heard them directly.</strong> At the hearing, the complainant stated the allegations were &ldquo;totally false&rdquo;, with her mother supporting her.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The State stepped back.</strong> The Additional Public Prosecutor recorded no serious objection to the grant of bail.</div>
</div>

<h2>The order and its conditions</h2>
<p>Bail was granted on a personal bond of Rs 10,000 with one surety: conditions include cooperating with the investigation, maintaining a verified address and mobile number, no inducement or tampering, appearance when directed, and reporting to the local police station on the first Monday of each month. The cancellation clause is specific &mdash; a fresh FIR, DD entry or complaint against the petitioner opens the door for the State to seek recall.</p>

<h2>Reading the case in context</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Timing the successive application.</strong> The moment the principal witness&rsquo;s examination concludes without supporting the prosecution is the inflection point; filing before that risks another dismissal on unchanged facts.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Sole-breadwinner and family realities.</strong> In intra-family POCSO prosecutions, courts increasingly confront recantation; bail jurisprudence responds through the change-of-circumstances route rather than by prejudging truth.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For complainants.</strong> Resiling has consequences beyond bail &mdash; perjury exposure and the continuing trial &mdash; and the deposition stage, not the bail hearing, is where the case is truly decided.</div>
</div>
<p>With trials under the BNSS now listing victim testimony early in POCSO matters, orders like this one underline why the first prosecution witnesses often determine the custody question long before judgment.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports an order of the High Court of Delhi for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
